    <!-- Which law -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="law-heading">
      <h2 id="law-heading">Which Rules Apply to Your Temporary Alimony Claim?</h2>
      <p>
        The law that governs your marriage decides how and when you can ask, so settle this first. Federal
        Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, in force since 15 April 2025, applies to UAE citizens where one or both
        spouses are Muslim, and to non-citizens unless one of them insists on applying his or her own law or
        they agree on another permitted law (Article 1). Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022, in force since
        1 February 2023, applies to non-Muslim foreigners living in the UAE and non-Muslim citizens, unless
        one spouse adheres to the law of their home country (Article 1).
      </p>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Muslim track (2024 law)</h3>
          <p>
            Temporary alimony is a request inside an alimony case. You do not have to file for divorce
            first.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Civil track, other emirates</h3>
          <p>
            Alimony follows the divorce judgment. Article 10 of the executive regulation, Cabinet Resolution
            No. 122 of 2023, adds a temporary alimony request until the financial claims are decided.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court</h3>
          <p>
            Divorce at the first hearing, then a post-divorce financial application, with provisional alimony
            decided by interim order in between.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

